REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0014"],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7"]}

**New Evidence**
- Xi’s February 16 call with Macron is the clearest direct 2022 Xi statement favoring negotiation on Ukraine.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Xi Jinping Speaks with French President Emmanuel Macron on the Phone","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-02-16","url":"https://www.fmprc.gov.cn/eng/zy/jj/bj2022/zyxw/202202/t20220217_10643016.html","claim":"Xi said relevant parties should stick to political settlement and seek a comprehensive settlement of the Ukraine issue through dialogue and consultation.","locator":"lines 88-89","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official readout of a dated Xi phone call.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period diplomatic conduct.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi as head of state and party leader; still a diplomatic readout rather than an operational order.","contrary_evidence":["It is a general diplomatic appeal, not proof of sustained peace leverage.","It did not prevent later coercive moves around Taiwan or continued alignment with Russia."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7"]}

- Xi’s March 18 call with Biden explicitly rejected battlefield confrontation and framed peace as the priority.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Xi Jinping: Countries should not come to the point of meeting on the battlefield. Conflict and confrontation are not in anyone's interest. Peace and security are what the international community should treasure the most.","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-03-18","url":"https://www.mfa.gov.cn/eng/gjhdq_665435/3376_665447/3432_664920/3435_664926/202203/t20220318_10653158.html","claim":"Xi said the Ukraine crisis was not something China wanted to see and that countries should not come to the point of meeting on the battlefield.","locator":"opening paragraph / lead summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official Xi readout with explicit peace language and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 Ukraine crisis period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi.","contrary_evidence":["The call is declaratory and does not show concrete leverage over Russia.","It coexisted with later Chinese non-condemnation of Russia at the UN."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Xi’s February 25 call with Putin supported negotiations and repeated China’s sovereignty/UN Charter line, but it also accepted Russia’s security framing.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Xi Jinping Speaks with Russian President Vladimir Putin on the Phone","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://www.fmprc.gov.cn/mfa_eng/gjhdq_665435/3265_665445/3220_664352/3222_664356/202202/t20220225_10645701.html","claim":"Xi said China would support Russia and Ukraine resolving the issue through negotiations and reiterated China's consistent position on respecting sovereignty and the UN Charter.","locator":"lead paragraph / summary of Xi's remarks","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official readout of a dated Xi-Putin phone call.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-period response to the invasion's start.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi, though the statement is mediated through a bilateral readout.","contrary_evidence":["The call did not publicly condemn Russia's invasion.","It echoed Russian security talking points about NATO and legitimacy of Russian concerns."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.3"]}

- Wang Yi’s five-point Ukraine position is official Chinese policy evidence under Xi-era leadership, emphasizing sovereignty, restraint, and talks.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Wang Yi Expounds China's Five-Point Position on the Current Ukraine Issue","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-02-26","url":"https://www.fmprc.gov.cn/eng/wjbzhd/202202/t20220226_10645855.html","claim":"China said sovereignty and territorial integrity should be respected, parties should exercise restraint, and diplomatic efforts toward a peaceful settlement should be supported.","locator":"lines 67-73","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official policy statement, dated immediately after the invasion began.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact early-2022 Ukraine crisis fit.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Xi as top leader of the Chinese state; this is state policy, not a personal command act.","contrary_evidence":["The statement also gave rhetorical space to Russia's 'legitimate security concerns'.","It is not a neutral condemnation of aggression."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- China abstained in the Security Council vote on the initial Ukraine resolution, which undercuts the peace language.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia vetoes U.N. Security action on Ukraine as China abstains","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/russia-vetoes-un-security-action-on-ukraine-china-abstains-2772930","claim":"China abstained when the Security Council voted on a resolution that would have deplored Russia's invasion of Ukraine.","locator":"lead paragraph / Reuters opening","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting on a major UN vote with direct diplomatic consequences.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact invasion-day international response.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Xi-era state policy, not a personal vote by Xi.","contrary_evidence":["Abstention is less supportive than a veto or yes vote for Russia.","China may have wanted to preserve room for diplomacy."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.9"]}

- China again abstained when the General Assembly condemned the invasion, saying the resolution would not be helpful.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"U.N. General Assembly in historic vote denounces Russia over Ukraine invasion","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-03-02","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/un-general-assembly-set-to-censure-russia-over-ukraine-invasion-2775771","claim":"China abstained because it did not believe the resolution would be helpful and said actions should promote de-escalation and an early resumption of dialogue.","locator":"Reuters article lines 432-435 as captured in search/open output","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting directly quotes China's deputy UN ambassador on the abstention rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 war response fit.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect state attribution; this is China's UN line under Xi, not a personal Xi statement.","contrary_evidence":["The stance did not join the international condemnation of aggression.","It can be framed as pro-dialogue, but it still avoided accountability language."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

- The August 3 Taiwan Office statement is overt coercive rhetoric against separatists.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Spokesperson for Taiwan Work Office of CPC Central Committee makes remarks on punishing die-hard 'Taiwan independence' elements","publisher":"English.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/news/topnews/202208/03/content_WS62e9d976c6d02e533532ed4a.html","claim":"The statement says die-hard 'Taiwan independence' elements severely jeopardized peace and stability across the Taiwan Straits and must be severely punished in accordance with the law.","locator":"lines 45-52","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official Chinese party-state publication with explicit coercive language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 Taiwan crisis period.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Xi-era governance and Taiwan policy; the statement is institutional rather than personally signed by Xi.","contrary_evidence":["The document frames the measures as legal and defensive.","It does not itself describe an actual military operation."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.6"]}

- The August 10 Taiwan white paper explicitly refused to renounce force.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China releases white paper on Taiwan question, reunification in new era","publisher":"English.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-08-10","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/archive/whitepaper/202208/10/content_WS62f34f46c6d02e533532f0ac.html","claim":"China said it would work for peaceful reunification but would not renounce the use of force and reserved the option of taking all necessary measures.","locator":"lines 63-65","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official white paper with explicit force reservation language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 policy statement after the Taiwan crisis escalated.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi-era state policy and party leadership; not a spontaneous military field decision.","contrary_evidence":["The same text emphasizes peaceful reunification as the stated preference.","It is a declaratory doctrine, not proof of imminent attack intent."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Reuters’ Taiwan crisis coverage shows the largest live-fire drills around Taiwan and direct risk escalation after Pelosi's visit.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Furious China fires missiles near Taiwan in drills after Pelosi visit","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/suspected-drones-over-taiwan-cyber-attacks-after-pelosi-visit-2862740","claim":"China deployed large-scale military drills and fired live missiles near Taiwan, sharply raising the risk of regional miscalculation after Pelosi's visit.","locator":"lead paragraph / Reuters opening","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire item on a major crisis event with direct operational details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 Taiwan crisis window.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi's governing system and Taiwan policy, though not a quoted Xi order.","contrary_evidence":["The drills were conventional rather than nuclear.","They can be read as signaling as well as coercion."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.4","2B.8","2B.10"]}

- China then halted military dialogue and other cooperation with the U.S., cutting crisis channels.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China halts high-level military dialogue with U.S., suspends other cooperation","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-08-05","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/beijing-halts-highlevel-military-dialogue-with-us-suspends-other-cooperation-2864888","claim":"China said it was halting senior-level military dialogue with the United States and suspending other cooperation, including climate talks, in retaliation for Pelosi's Taiwan visit.","locator":"lead paragraph / Reuters opening","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting on a specific suspension of channels and mechanisms.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 crisis aftermath.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Xi-era state control over the U.S. relationship and military channels.","contrary_evidence":["Suspending dialogue may have been intended as temporary punishment rather than permanent rupture.","This does not prove all backchannel communication ceased."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across official MFA and gov.cn material, Reuters wire coverage, UN voting records, and China’s Taiwan policy texts.
- I retained only non-duplicative items that added a distinct claim about peace, restraint, coercion, dialogue, or crisis management.
- I rejected later retrospective or post-2022 sources unless they were only being used as a clean mirror for a 2022 document, and I avoided broad commentary that did not add a ruler-attributed claim.
- I rejected duplicate republishes of the same Reuters wires once the underlying Reuters reporting was already captured.
- I did not re-fetch the local structured UCDP/SIPRI priors already supplied in the prompt; I used them only as context for the 2022 baseline.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence of Xi personally authorizing or restraining specific military moves in the Taiwan crisis, beyond official readouts and policy texts.
- Better evidence on internal deliberation, actual command authority, and whether Xi overruled hawks or empowered them.
- More direct evidence on whether China took concrete steps to mediate the Ukraine war beyond declaratory calls for talks and abstentions at the UN.
- Better documentation of civilian-protection practices during the Taiwan drills, though no actual armed conflict occurred.
- More granular evidence on whether the dialogue suspensions were paired with alternative de-escalation channels.

chapter_notes.3B:
**Reused Evidence**
None.

**New Evidence**
1. OHCHR’s Xinjiang assessment is the strongest independent baseline for 3B because it ties the repression framework to large-scale arbitrary detention and possible crimes against humanity.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OHCHR Assessment of human rights concerns in the Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region, People's Republic of China","publisher":"UN Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights","publication_date":"2022-08-31","url":"https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3985699/files/1352584.pdf","claim":"OHCHR concluded that serious human rights violations were committed in Xinjiang and that the extent of arbitrary and discriminatory detention of Uyghurs and other predominantly Muslim groups may constitute international crimes, in particular crimes against humanity.","locator":"pp. 44-45, paras. 143-149 / lines 2065-2116","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN human-rights assessment using official documents, interviews, and open-source corroboration.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the repression architecture that remained the operative baseline in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi-era party-state governance and the Xinjiang policy framework, not to a single public Xi order in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The report centers the earlier 2017-2019 period rather than a new 2022 operation.","It is a legal-human-rights assessment, not a criminal judgment."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.10"]}

2. Xi’s July 2022 Xinjiang tour is the clearest primary-source window into his own domestic-safety framing and minority-control priorities.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Xi stresses implementing Party's policies on Xinjiang, highlights stability, security","publisher":"gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-07-16","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/news/topnews/202207/16/content_WS62d2237fc6d02e533532e11e.html","claim":"Xi said the overarching goal in Xinjiang was social stability and lasting security, that Islam in China must be Chinese in orientation, that the normal religious needs of believers should be ensured, and that the Xinjiang Production and Construction Corps should act as a stabilizer for border security.","locator":"lines 46-49, 68-69, 88-95, 97-107","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official readout of Xi's own inspection tour and instructions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period ruler speech and policy signal.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi as general secretary, state president, and CMC chairman.","contrary_evidence":["The language is officially framed as stability and development rather than overt repression.","It does not itself prove every downstream abuse or local enforcement action."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}

3. Amnesty’s 2022 review captures the zero-COVID backlash, the Urumqi trigger, and the state’s limited retreat.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Amnesty International Report 2022/23; The State of the World's Human Rights; China 2022","publisher":"ecoi.net mirroring Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-03-27","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089448.html","claim":"Amnesty reported that China kept a zero-Covid policy for most of 2022, that protests spread to at least 20 cities after the Urumqi apartment-block fire, and that the government then announced easing of Covid-19 restrictions.","locator":"lines 38-46","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct NGO annual report covering the target year with specific incident chronology.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the full target year and the immediate late-2022 crisis response.","ruler_attribution":"State policy under Xi, though the report mostly describes system-level conduct rather than a single personal order.","contrary_evidence":["The authorities denied that locked doors prevented escape.","The easing shows partial policy retreat rather than pure continuity."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.6","3B.9","3B.10"]}

4. Amnesty also documents direct coercion against peaceful protest, including app manipulation, arrests, and censorship.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Amnesty International Report 2022/23; The State of the World's Human Rights; China 2022","publisher":"ecoi.net mirroring Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-03-27","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089448.html","claim":"Amnesty reported that authorities manipulated the Covid health-status app to unduly restrict movement and assembly, arrested Peng Lifa after he displayed anti-zero-Covid banners in Beijing, removed footage and keywords from social media, and detained people for participating in peaceful protests after the Urumqi fire.","locator":"lines 49-57","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct annual report with multiple contemporaneous protest-related incidents and state responses.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 protest wave and congress-period context.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi-era governance and security apparatus behavior; not every arrest is personally ordered by Xi.","contrary_evidence":["Officials later eased some restrictions.","The report does not quantify every detention retained by year-end."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

5. Amnesty’s account of human-rights defenders shows detention, incommunicado confinement, and ill-treatment rather than restraint or remedy.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Amnesty International Report 2022/23; The State of the World's Human Rights; China 2022","publisher":"ecoi.net mirroring Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-03-27","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089448.html","claim":"Amnesty reported that authorities imprisoned human rights defenders and subjected them to harsh conditions and torture or other ill-treatment, including Yu Wensheng's account of pepper-spraying, forced immobilization, and denial of food, and Wang Jianbing's incommunicado detention and ill-treatment.","locator":"lines 60-66","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct NGO reporting on named cases with concrete custodial abuse allegations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 with adjacent 2023 publication summarizing the year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Chinese security and prison system under Xi-era rule, though specific custody acts are local/provincial.","contrary_evidence":["These are named cases, not a complete census of all detainees.","The report does not show the exact chain of command for each abuse."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.6"]}

6. Amnesty’s Xinjiang section is a compact high-value source on systematic repression, detention, and coercive governance.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Amnesty International Report 2022/23; The State of the World's Human Rights; China 2022","publisher":"ecoi.net mirroring Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-03-27","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089448.html","claim":"Amnesty said systematic repression of ethnic minorities in Xinjiang continued, that many thousands still appeared to be detained in internment camps, prisons, or other facilities, and that political indoctrination, physical and psychological torture, sexual violence, forced labor, and enforced disappearances were widely reported.","locator":"lines 72-81","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct annual report with explicit synthesis of multiple evidence streams and clear year coverage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 and its immediate policy aftermath.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi-era central policy and enforcement architecture, not to a single local official alone.","contrary_evidence":["The report notes official claims that camps had closed, but says many people were still believed detained.","Some details are necessarily inferential because access to the region was highly restricted."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}

7. Amnesty’s Tibet section adds another distinct repression channel: arbitrary detention, harsh sentences, and ill-treatment of writers and activists.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Amnesty International Report 2022/23; The State of the World's Human Rights; China 2022","publisher":"ecoi.net mirroring Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-03-27","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089448.html","claim":"Amnesty reported that ethnic Tibetans faced discrimination and restrictions on religion, expression, association, and peaceful assembly; that six Tibetan writers and activists received prison terms of four to 14 years; and that some had been arbitrarily detained previously and suffered beatings, poor detention conditions, and other ill-treatment.","locator":"lines 86-91","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct annual report with specific named cases and clear target-year fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 with immediate aftermath published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the central CCP governance model under Xi, with Tibetan-area implementation by local and regional security organs.","contrary_evidence":["These are selected cases, not a full incident database.","The report does not prove that every sentence was ordered personally by Xi."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.7","3B.10"]}

8. Amnesty’s Hong Kong section shows the national-security clampdown persisted through 2022 and directly affected protest, association, and media freedom.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Amnesty International Report 2022/23; The State of the World's Human Rights; China 2022","publisher":"ecoi.net mirroring Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-03-27","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089448.html","claim":"Amnesty reported that Hong Kong authorities continued their crackdown on the pro-democracy movement, that journalists and civil society actors were prosecuted or harassed under the National Security Law and other repressive laws, and that police arrested people for peaceful protest and intensifed surveillance.","locator":"lines 79-81, 49-57, 54-57","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct annual report with multiple independent examples of repression in Hong Kong during the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 with late-2022 legislative and policing effects.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Xi-era central control over Hong Kong, though much enforcement is carried out by SAR and security organs.","contrary_evidence":["Hong Kong still had some separate legal institutions and public reporting.","The report does not claim Xi personally directed each arrest."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}

9. Amnesty’s women’s-rights section gives a limited favorable counterpoint: formal legal change and punishment of some attackers, alongside ongoing censorship and violence.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Amnesty International Report 2022/23; The State of the World's Human Rights; China 2022","publisher":"ecoi.net mirroring Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-03-27","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089448.html","claim":"Amnesty reported that the top legislature adopted an amended women's-rights law, that 28 people were sentenced for the Tangshan restaurant attack, and that the Ministry of Public Security launched a campaign to investigate trafficking of women and children, even as violence against women and censorship of discussion remained widespread.","locator":"lines 97-103","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct annual report containing both a remedial measure and its continuing limits.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 with a late-2022 legal and law-enforcement response.","ruler_attribution":"This is a state response under Xi-era rule, but the report does not show a unique Xi personal initiative.","contrary_evidence":["Violence against women remained widespread.","Authorities censored public discussion of the Tangshan assault and arrested a journalist who publicized it."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.7","3B.9"]}

10. Human Rights Watch independently corroborates the coercive zero-Covid system and the harm it caused.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: China","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/china","claim":"HRW said authorities maintained a strict zero-Covid policy in 2022, that residents in places such as Shanghai were confined for weeks, that access to health care and food was badly impeded, and that an unknown number of people died after being denied medical treatment for non-Covid illnesses.","locator":"Covid-19 section, lines 121-125","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent human-rights annual report with clear, specific 2022 examples.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly addresses the target year and its domestic-safety consequences.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi-era national policy and implementation, not to a single local lockdown alone.","contrary_evidence":["HRW notes the policy prevented some Covid deaths and illnesses.","The report focuses on consequence, not a legal finding of intent."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.6","3B.9"]}

11. HRW’s Hong Kong and Xinjiang sections add independent corroboration for repression, surveillance, and mass detention.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: China","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/china","claim":"HRW reported that Hong Kong authorities attacked press freedom, expanded surveillance, and arrested protesters, while in Xinjiang it said an estimated one million Uyghurs were wrongfully detained in political education camps, pretrial detention centers, and prisons and that roughly half a million had been sentenced.","locator":"Hong Kong section lines 90-102; Xinjiang section lines 105-107","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent human-rights annual report with concrete Hong Kong and Xinjiang claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly describes the 2022 domestic-safety environment.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi-era central authority and the security apparatus, though not every act was personally ordered by Xi.","contrary_evidence":["The report distinguishes Hong Kong and Xinjiang and does not claim every abuse was centrally micromanaged.","It is a high-level annual synthesis rather than case-by-case proof."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}

12. Reuters’ late-November coverage is the clearest contemporaneous reporting on the protest wave and security response.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China tightens security after rare protests against COVID curbs","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-11-27","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/china-reports-another-daily-record-of-covid-cases-as-protests-ripple-across-china-2952419","claim":"Reuters reported that police stopped and searched people at protest sites in Shanghai and Beijing, that protests spread from several cities to dozens of university campuses, and that the wave of civil disobedience was unprecedented since Xi took power.","locator":"lines 428-433, 452-454","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting on a major contemporaneous domestic-security episode.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 protest/crisis window.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Xi-era domestic governance; the reporting is on state response rather than a personal Xi quote.","contrary_evidence":["The protests were nonviolent civil disobedience rather than organized armed unrest.","The report also notes officials framed the measures as lawful public-health enforcement."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

13. Reuters then reported follow-on questioning and phone checks, showing the state moved quickly from crowd control to targeted follow-up.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Chinese authorities seek out COVID protesters","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-11-28","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/coronavirus/china-police-out-in-numbers-to-prevent-more-covid-protests-2953228","claim":"Reuters reported that Chinese authorities began inquiries into some weekend protesters, asked them for written accounts, and checked phones for VPN and Telegram use while police remained out in numbers on the streets.","locator":"lines 428-433, 451-455","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting with direct witness accounts and a clear time stamp.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact immediate aftermath of the November 2022 protests.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi-era public-security control over protest response; not a personally signed Xi order.","contrary_evidence":["The article does not quantify how many protesters were ultimately detained or disciplined.","Authorities may have presented the checks as lawful procedure."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

14. Reuters’ Shanghai lockdown-ends story is useful for the material harm side of the zero-Covid policy.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"After two months, a scarred Shanghai's COVID-19 lockdown ends","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-05-31","url":"https://uk.investing.com/news/coronavirus/after-two-months-a-scarred-shanghais-covid19-lockdown-ends-2659718","claim":"Reuters said Shanghai's draconian lockdown left residents frustrated and in economic loss, confined 25 million people for almost all of the lockdown, and forced many to struggle for food or medical care while families were separated and hundreds of thousands were sent to centralized quarantine.","locator":"lines 429-449","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting on the country’s largest and most visible lockdown episode.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 domestic-safety and coercive-control evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Xi because zero-Covid was a national policy championed under his leadership; local implementation still mattered.","contrary_evidence":["The story is a consequence report, not a direct order record.","Lockdowns were justified as public-health measures, not as deliberate political terror."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.6","3B.9"]}

15. The Urumqi-fire protest coverage shows the crisis trigger that made the repression and policy reversal politically visible.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Covid-19 in China: Urumqi to ease lockdowns amid unrest over deadly fire","publisher":"South China Morning Post","publication_date":"2022-11-26","url":"https://www.scmp.com/news/china/politics/article/3201106/covid-19-china-urumqi-ease-lockdowns-amid-unrest-over-deadly-fire","claim":"SCMP reported that Urumqi authorities said the city would lift coronavirus restrictions in phases after rare protests over a residential fire that killed 10 people, with videos showing crowds chanting for the lockdown to end.","locator":"article body / paragraphs on the phased easing and protests","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Regional outlet with Reuters-sourced field reporting on a highly time-sensitive domestic-security event.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact late-2022 crisis trigger.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Xi-era policy because the unrest reflected national zero-Covid governance and its local enforcement.","contrary_evidence":["The article is about a local emergency and protest response, not direct national command.","The city said restrictions would be eased in phases, so the response was not only coercive."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.8","3B.9"]}

16. The 2022 State Department report provides a blunt official baseline on authoritarian control and killings.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Reports on Human Rights Practices: China (Includes Hong Kong, Macau, and Tibet) - China","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://2021-2025.state.gov/report/custom/e366b8da35/","claim":"The State Department described the PRC as an authoritarian state in which the CCP is the paramount authority, said Xi continued to hold the three most powerful positions, and reported numerous claims that the government or its agents committed arbitrary or unlawful killings.","locator":"Executive Summary / Section 1.a","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary U.S. government annual human-rights report with explicit attribution and physical-integrity baseline.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the 2022 reporting year and directly names Xi’s authority bundle.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Xi because the report identifies him in the top three offices and frames the CCP as the center of authority.","contrary_evidence":["The report is a broad country baseline, not a case-specific finding on Xi personally ordering each abuse.","It aggregates many issues and does not isolate 2022-only incidents."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.10"]}

17. The Tibet section of the same State Department report adds a useful accountability and oversight baseline for a minority region under Xi-era rule.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Reports on Human Rights Practices: China (Includes Hong Kong, Macau, and Tibet) - Tibet","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://2021-2025.state.gov/report/custom/f92257ead0/","claim":"The report said the CCP Central Committee oversees Tibet policy, that ultimate authority rests with the Politburo and Standing Committee in Beijing, and that significant issues included forced disappearances, torture, arbitrary detention, political prisoners, censorship, and substantial interference with peaceful assembly and association.","locator":"Executive Summary / Section 1","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary U.S. government annual report with explicit attribution and detailed physical-safety concerns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the 2022 reporting year and explains the national decision structure relevant to Xi.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly but materially attributable to Xi-era central authority because the report identifies Beijing as the locus of ultimate power.","contrary_evidence":["This is a regional baseline, not proof of Xi's direct involvement in every detention or beating.","The report says civilian authorities maintained control over the security forces, which complicates simple personal attribution."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.7","3B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across OHCHR, Amnesty, Human Rights Watch, Reuters, South China Morning Post, Xinhua/gov.cn, and the U.S. State Department.
- I retained 10 distinct URLs and 17 source-claim units because they added distinct claims rather than repeating the same incident through duplicate wire republishes.
- I rejected duplicate Reuters syndications, image-only Reuters Connect items, and current 2025/2026 Amnesty/HRW pages that were outside the 2022 target year.
- I also rejected older 2018-2020 country-report pages when a 2022-report source or a stronger 2022 annual report already covered the same ground.
- Broad economic or foreign-policy pieces were discarded when they did not add direct domestic physical-safety evidence.
- I did not re-fetch the local structured priors already supplied in the prompt; I used them only as baseline context.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence of Xi personally ordering or overruling specific domestic security operations in 2022 is still thin.
- Internal chain-of-command evidence for the protest crackdown, Hong Kong security actions, and Xinjiang implementation is limited.
- The record on incitement is mostly policy framing and assimilation language, not explicit personal hatred or violence-encouraging rhetoric.
- We have strong evidence of systematic repression, but not a complete incident-level count of all detentions, deaths, or custody abuse in 2022.
- More search could still help on command responsibility and local implementation, but the big-picture domestic-safety pattern is already well supported.

